21621,11,11453,10,57212,7,16,1106,7633,1188,19,11,15,29621,7,15,11,23,29621,7,23,11,23,29621,21621,11,3691,51412,13571,10,57212,7,16,1106,7633,1188,15,11,18,29621,7,19,11,16,16,29621,7,23,11,18,29621,21621,11,3691,51412,15521,10,57212,7,16,1106,1502,445,19,44970,23,11,20,13,20,701,36,317,24157,6405,60,151645,198,151644,77091,198],"messages":[{"content":"Solve the problem step by step. Give your final answer in \\boxed{...}.\n\nBy joining alternate vertices of a regular hexagon with edges $4$ inches long, two equilateral triangles are formed, as shown. What is the area, in square inches, of the region that is common to the two triangles? Express your answer in simplest radical form. [asy]\ndraw((0,3)--(0,8)--(4,11)--(8,8)--(8,3)--(4,0)--cycle,black+linewidth(1));\ndraw((4,0)--(0,8)--(8,8)--cycle, black+dashed+linewidth(1));\ndraw((0,3)--(4,11)--(8,3)--cycle, black+dotted+linewidth(1));\nlabel(\"4\",(8,5.5),E);\n[/asy]","role":"user"}],"rendered_sha256":"5b6e4fc7504f83664d0c9030035887b3adcc7132fbd93a6780ac61da635a28c2"},"seconds":32.10161757469177,"seed":805344318208671409,"stop":"eos","text":"To solve the problem, we will first understand the geometric configuration and then calculate the area of the common region between the two equilateral triangles formed by joining alternate vertices of a regular hexagon with edge length 4 inches.\n\n1. **Identify the Geometry:**\n   - A regular hexagon can be divided into 6 equilateral triangles by drawing lines from the center to each vertex.\n   - Joining alternate vertices of the hexagon forms two larger equilateral triangles. Each of these triangles shares a common central region.\n\n2. **Calculate the Side Length of the Larger Equilateral Triangles:**\n   - The side length of the hexagon is 4 inches.\n   - The side length of the larger equilateral triangles is twice the side length of the hexagon (since they span two sides of the hexagon), which is \\(4 \\times 2 = 8\\) inches.\n\n3. **Calculate the Area of the Larger Equilateral Triangles:**\n   - The area \\(A\\) of an equilateral triangle with side length \\(s\\) is given by the formula:\n     \\[\n     A = \\frac{s^2 \\sqrt{3}}{4}\n     \\]\n   - For a side length of 8 inches:\n     \\[\n     A = \\frac{8^2 \\sqrt{3}}{4} = \\frac{64 \\sqrt{3}}{4} = 16 \\sqrt{3}\n     \\]\n   - Therefore, the area of each larger equilateral triangle is \\(16 \\sqrt{3}\\) square inches.\n\n4. **Identify the Common Region:**\n   - The common region shared by the two larger equilateral triangles is another equilateral triangle.\n   - This common triangle is formed by the central equilateral triangle of the hexagon, which has a side length equal to the side length of the hexagon, which is 4 inches.\n\n5. **Calculate the Area of the Common Region:**\n   - Using the area formula for an equilateral triangle with side length 4 inches:\n     \\[\n     A = \\frac{4^2 \\sqrt{3}}{4} = \\frac{16 \\sqrt{3}}{4} = 4 \\sqrt{3}\n     \\]\n   - Therefore, the area of the common region is \\(4 \\sqrt{3}\\) square inches.\n\nThus, the area of the region that is common to the two triangles is \\(\\boxed{4\\sqrt{3}}\\)."},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{4\\sqrt{3}}","correct":false,"format":"boxed","parseable":true}}
